proofs.proof_tex:
Let \(\widetilde{\mathcal M}_n\) be the class in the proposed statement and fix \((P,m_0,m_1,p)\in\widetilde{\mathcal M}_n\).  By its definition, this quadruple satisfies every member condition of \texttt{def:public-weak-overlap-class} except that the two shell-richness inequalities are not imposed.  We first record the shell consequences used below, thereby verifying that neither omitted condition is needed.

Put \(F(t)=P\{e(X)\leq t\}\).  By \texttt{ass:positivity}, \(P\{e(X)=0\}=0\).  Hence the dyadic shells \((2^{-j-1}t,2^{-j}t]\), \(j\geq0\), partition \(\{0<e(X)\leq t\}\).  Applying \texttt{ass:uniform-shell} at \(2^{-j}t\) and summing the resulting geometric series gives, for \(0<t\leq\lambda_0\),
\[
c_-t^\kappa
\leq F(t)
\leq c_+t^\kappa\sum_{j=0}^{\infty}2^{-j\kappa}
=\frac{c_+}{1-2^{-\kappa}}t^\kappa.                    \qquad\text{(1)}
\]
The lower bound uses only the single shell \(\{t/2<e(X)\leq t\}\).

For \(0<\lambda\leq\lambda_0\), let \(J\) be the largest nonnegative integer such that \(2^J\lambda\leq\lambda_0\).  Decomposing at \(\lambda,2\lambda,\ldots,2^J\lambda\), using (1) below \(\lambda\), and using \texttt{ass:uniform-shell} on every subsequent shell yields
\[
\begin{split}
E_P\!\left[(e(X)\vee\lambda)^{-1}\right]
&\leq \frac{F(\lambda)}{\lambda}
+c_+2^\kappa\lambda^{\kappa-1}
  \sum_{j=0}^{J-1}2^{j(\kappa-1)}
+\frac{2}{\lambda_0}.                                  \qquad\text{(2)}
\end{split}
\]
Indeed, on \(\{2^j\lambda<e(X)\leq2^{j+1}\lambda\}\), the inverse is at most \((2^j\lambda)^{-1}\), while the shell probability is at most \(c_+(2^{j+1}\lambda)^\kappa\); on the remaining event \(\{e(X)>2^J\lambda\}\), maximality of \(J\) gives \((2^J\lambda)^{-1}<2/\lambda_0\).  If \(0<\kappa<1\), (1) also gives the lower bound \(F(\lambda)/\lambda\geq c_-\lambda^{\kappa-1}\).  If \(\kappa=1\), each of the \(J\) shells in (2) contributes at least \(c_-\) to the expectation, because its mass is at least \(c_-2^{j+1}\lambda\) and its inverse is at least \((2^{j+1}\lambda)^{-1}\).  If \(\kappa>1\), the inverse is at least one because \(e(X)\vee\lambda\leq1\).  Evaluating the geometric sum in (2), and using \(1+J\asymp1+\log(\lambda_0/\lambda)\) when \(\kappa=1\), proves
\[
E_P\!\left[(e(X)\vee\lambda)^{-1}\right]
\asymp v_\kappa(\lambda)^2,                             \qquad\text{(3)}
\]
with constants depending only on \((c_-,c_+,\kappa,\lambda_0)\).  Thus (1)--(3) establish on \(\widetilde{\mathcal M}_n\) precisely the shell estimates used from \texttt{lem:uniform-shell-calculus} on \(\mathcal M_n\).

Write
\[
\Delta=g_1-g_0,\qquad h_a=m_a-g_a,\qquad
\varepsilon_a=Y-g_a(X)\ \text{on }\{D=a\},
\qquad e_\lambda=e\vee\lambda.
\]
The causal target remains the given identifying functional \(\tau(P)=E_P[\Delta(X)]\); \texttt{ass:consistency} and \texttt{ass:ignorability} justify its interpretation as the full-population ATE and do not define the estimator.  By \texttt{ass:right-overlap}, \(e(X)\in[0,1-\eta_+/2]\) almost surely.  Projection onto this interval and the map \(z\mapsto z\vee\lambda\) are contractions, so
\[
|p^\circ-e|\leq|p-e|,\qquad
|q_\lambda-e_\lambda|\leq|p^\circ-e|\leq|p-e|,
\qquad 1-p^\circ\geq\eta_+/2.                            \qquad\text{(4)}
\]
Every division below is legitimate: \(\lambda>0\), \texttt{ass:positivity} gives \(e(X)>0\) almost surely, and \texttt{ass:right-overlap} gives \(1-e(X)\geq\eta_+>0\).

Substitution into \texttt{def:clipped-score} gives the exact identity
\[
\psi_\lambda
=\Delta+h_1-h_0+\frac{D(\varepsilon_1-h_1)}{q_\lambda}
-\frac{(1-D)(\varepsilon_0-h_0)}{1-p^\circ}.             \qquad\text{(5)}
\]
Add and subtract the true clipped treated denominator and the true control denominator.  Pointwise,
\[
\begin{split}
\psi_\lambda-\tau(P)
={}&A+B_1+B_0+C_1+C_0+R_1+R_0,\\
A={}&\Delta-\tau(P)+\frac{D\varepsilon_1}{e_\lambda}
-\frac{(1-D)\varepsilon_0}{1-e},\\
B_1={}&h_1\left(1-\frac{D}{e_\lambda}\right),
\qquad
B_0=-h_0\left(1-\frac{1-D}{1-e}\right),\\
C_1={}&D\varepsilon_1\left(\frac{1}{q_\lambda}-\frac{1}{e_\lambda}\right),\\
C_0={}&-(1-D)\varepsilon_0
\left(\frac{1}{1-p^\circ}-\frac{1}{1-e}\right),\\
R_1={}&Dh_1\left(\frac{1}{e_\lambda}-\frac{1}{q_\lambda}\right),
\qquad
R_0=(1-D)h_0\left(\frac{1}{1-p^\circ}-\frac{1}{1-e}\right).
\end{split}                                               \qquad\text{(6)}
\]
The definitions of \(g_0\) and \(g_1\) imply \(E_P(\varepsilon_a\mid D=a,X)=0\).  Consequently
\(E_PA=E_PC_1=E_PC_0=E_PB_0=0\), while
\[
E_PB_1
=E_P\!\left[h_1(X)\left(1-\frac{e(X)}{e_\lambda(X)}\right)\right].
\]
By Cauchy--Schwarz, \texttt{ass:public-g1-radius}, and (1),
\[
|E_PB_1|
\leq r_{1n}P\{e(X)<\lambda\}^{1/2}
\leq C r_{1n}\lambda^{\kappa/2}.                        \qquad\text{(7)}
\]

We next bound the centered empirical terms.  Minkowski's inequality, \texttt{ass:bounded-treatment-effect-signal}, the two conditional residual-variance assumptions, \texttt{ass:right-overlap}, and (3) give
\[
\begin{split}
\|A\|_{P,2}
&\leq \|\Delta-E_P\Delta\|_{P,2}
+\left\|\frac{D\varepsilon_1}{e_\lambda}\right\|_{P,2}
+\left\|\frac{(1-D)\varepsilon_0}{1-e}\right\|_{P,2}\\
&\leq \sqrt M
+\sigma_1 E_P[e_\lambda^{-1}]^{1/2}
+\sigma_0\eta_+^{-1/2}
\leq C v_\kappa(\lambda).                               \qquad\text{(8)}
\end{split}
\]
Here
\[
E_P\!\left[\frac{D\varepsilon_1^2}{e_\lambda^2}\right]
\leq\sigma_1^2E_P\!\left[\frac{e}{e_\lambda^2}\right]
\leq\sigma_1^2E_P[e_\lambda^{-1}],
\]
and the analogous control expectation is at most \(\sigma_0^2/\eta_+\).  Conditional on \(X\),
\[
E_P\!\left[h_1^2\left(1-\frac{D}{e_\lambda}\right)^2\middle|X\right]
\leq h_1^2(1+\lambda^{-1}),
\]
whereas the control counterpart is at most a constant multiple of \(h_0^2\).  Thus the two public regression certificates imply
\[
\|B_1\|_{P,2}\leq r_{1n}\sqrt{1+\lambda^{-1}},
\qquad \|B_0\|_{P,2}\leq C r_{0n}.                      \qquad\text{(9)}
\]
Using (4), \(e\leq e_\lambda\), \texttt{ass:public-e-radius}, and the conditional residual-variance bounds gives
\[
\begin{split}
E_P[C_1^2]
&\leq\sigma_1^2E_P\!\left[
\frac{e(p-e)^2}{q_\lambda^2e_\lambda^2}\right]
\leq\frac{\sigma_1^2r_{en}^2}{\lambda^3},\\
E_P[C_0^2]&\leq C\sigma_0^2r_{en}^2.                    \qquad\text{(10)}
\end{split}
\]
The remainders require no unassumed product second moment.  Conditioning on \(X\), applying (4), and then applying Cauchy--Schwarz to the two certified \(L^2(P_X)\) errors yields
\[
E_P|R_1|\leq\frac{E_P(|h_1||p-e|)}{\lambda}
\leq\frac{r_{1n}r_{en}}{\lambda},
\qquad
E_P|R_0|\leq C r_{0n}r_{en}.                             \qquad\text{(11)}
\]

For an i.i.d. sample and every square-integrable centered variable \(Z\),
\[
E_P|\mathbb P_nZ|
\leq\{E_P[(\mathbb P_nZ)^2]\}^{1/2}
=\frac{\|Z\|_{P,2}}{\sqrt n}.                           \qquad\text{(12)}
\]
Apply (12) to the centered versions of \(A,B_1,B_0,C_1,C_0\), retain the mean (7), and use \(E_P|\mathbb P_nR_a|\leq E_P|R_a|\).  Since every radius is at most \(\bar r\), the terms \(r_{0n}/\sqrt n\) and \(r_{en}/\sqrt n\) are absorbed by a constant multiple of \(n^{-1/2}\).  Equations (7)--(12) therefore imply
\[
E_P|\widehat\tau_\lambda-\tau(P)|
\leq C\left\{
\frac{v_\kappa(\lambda)}{\sqrt n}
+\frac{r_{1n}}{\sqrt{n\lambda}}
+\frac{r_{en}}{\sqrt n\,\lambda^{3/2}}
+r_{1n}\lambda^{\kappa/2}
+\frac{r_{1n}r_{en}}{\lambda}
+r_{0n}r_{en}\right\}.                                 \qquad\text{(13)}
\]

We now insert \(\lambda=\lambda_n^\star\).  First suppose the cap in \texttt{def:oracle-threshold} does not bind, and write \(L_n\) for its active maximum.  Since \(L_n\geq b_{o,n}=n^{-1/(\kappa+1)}\),
\[
\frac{r_{1n}}{\sqrt{nL_n}}
\leq r_{1n}L_n^{\kappa/2}.                               \qquad\text{(14)}
\]
Since \(L_n\geq b_{e,n}=r_{en}^{2/(\kappa+2)}\),
\[
\frac{r_{en}}{\sqrt nL_n^{3/2}}
\leq\frac{L_n^{(\kappa-1)/2}}{\sqrt n}
\leq
\begin{cases}
n^{-1/2}L_n^{-(1-\kappa)/2},&0<\kappa<1,\\
n^{-1/2},&\kappa\geq1,
\end{cases}                                              \qquad\text{(15)}
\]
and
\[
\frac{r_{1n}r_{en}}{L_n}
\leq r_{1n}r_{en}^{\kappa/(\kappa+2)}.                  \qquad\text{(16)}
\]
For \(0<\kappa<1\), the additional inequality \(L_n\geq b_{g,n}=(nr_{1n}^2)^{-1}\) gives
\[
\frac{v_\kappa(L_n)}{\sqrt n}
\leq n^{-\kappa/2}r_{1n}^{1-\kappa}
\leq Q_\kappa(n,r_{1n}).                                \qquad\text{(17)}
\]
At \(\kappa=1\), the same comparison inside the logarithm gives
\[
\frac{v_1(L_n)}{\sqrt n}
\leq Cn^{-1/2}\sqrt{1+\log_+(nr_{1n}^2)}
=C Q_1(n,r_{1n}),                                       \qquad\text{(18)}
\]
and for \(\kappa>1\) this term is \(Cn^{-1/2}\).  Finally, because a positive power of a maximum is bounded by the sum of the corresponding positive powers,
\[
\begin{split}
r_{1n}L_n^{\kappa/2}
\leq C\bigl\{&r_{1n}n^{-\kappa/[2(\kappa+1)]}
+r_{1n}r_{en}^{\kappa/(\kappa+2)}\\
&+\mathbf 1\{\kappa\leq1\}
n^{-\kappa/2}r_{1n}^{1-\kappa}\bigr\}.                \qquad\text{(19)}
\end{split}
\]
The last term in (19) is bounded by \(Q_\kappa(n,r_{1n})\), including its value \(n^{-1/2}\) when \(\kappa=1\).  Combining (13)--(19) with \texttt{def:tail-noise-rate} and \texttt{def:sharp-rate} proves
\[
E_P|\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^\star}-\tau(P)|\leq C\rho_n
\]
when the cap does not bind.

If the cap binds, \(\lambda_n^\star=\lambda_0\) and at least one active component is at least \(\lambda_0\).  If \(b_{o,n}\geq\lambda_0\), then \(n\leq\lambda_0^{-(\kappa+1)}\); bounded radii make every term in (13) a fixed multiple of \(n^{-1/2}\leq Q_\kappa(n,r_{1n})\).  If \(b_{e,n}\geq\lambda_0\), then \(r_{en}\geq\lambda_0^{(\kappa+2)/2}\); every fixed multiple of \(r_{1n}\) or \(r_{1n}r_{en}\) in (13) is then absorbed by a fixed multiple of \(r_{1n}r_{en}^{\kappa/(\kappa+2)}\), and the stochastic terms are fixed multiples of \(n^{-1/2}\).  Finally, if \(0<\kappa\leq1\) and \(b_{g,n}\geq\lambda_0\), then \(r_{1n}\leq(n\lambda_0)^{-1/2}\); every term in (13) containing \(r_{1n}\) is absorbed by a fixed multiple of \(n^{-1/2}\), using \(r_{en}\leq\bar r\).  The term \(r_{0n}r_{en}\) already occurs in \(\rho_n\).  These cases exhaust the active maximum, so uniformly over the broader class,
\[
\sup_{(P,m_0,m_1,p)\in\widetilde{\mathcal M}_n}
E_P\!\left|\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^\star}-\tau(P)\right|
\leq C\rho_n.                                           \qquad\text{(20)}
\]

The statistic is a measurable function of the observed sample, the public functions, and the certified radii; hence \texttt{def:estimator-class} gives \(\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^\star}\in\mathcal T_n\).  With \(W=|\widehat\tau_{\lambda_n^\star}-\tau(P)|\), Markov's inequality follows directly from
\[
E_PW\geq\frac{C\rho_n}{\delta}
P\!\left\{W>\frac{C\rho_n}{\delta}\right\}.
\]
Together with (20), this proves the displayed fixed-probability bound for every fixed \(\delta\in(0,1/4)\).  Finally, \(\mathcal M_n\subseteq\widetilde{\mathcal M}_n\) because \texttt{def:public-weak-overlap-class} adds the two richness conditions and changes no other member property.  Restricting either supremum in (20) and in the probability bound proves the original reservoir-rich conclusions as well.
